<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ProcessFlow, the smallest case: a service desk with two agents and one line (M/M/2) (PRO: ProcessFlow).

  Customers arrive at random, on average 1.6 a minute (Exponential, lambda 1.6) and wait in one first-come-first-served line for the next free one of two agents.
  Each service takes on average 1 minute (Exponential, lambda 1.0). ProcessFlow runs the event calendar and emits the event log, one event per row; the Recur
  after it turns the log into time-weighted averages, because the log's rows are unevenly spaced in time (a queue length that lasts three minutes and one that
  lasts a second must not count the same).
  Output columns, 14 per row:
    0 area under the queue length so far          1 area under the number of busy agents so far
    2, 3, 4 the Recur's memory for the next row: this event's time, queue length and busy agents (copies of columns 7, 11, 12)
    5 mean queue length so far (area / time)       6 mean busy agents so far (area / time)
    7 time of this event   8 customer number   9 station (0, the desk)   10 event type (0 arrive, 1 start, 2 finish, 3 exit)
    11 queue length after this event   12 agents busy after this event   13 customers in the system after this event
  An arrive event is the customer joining the line, so it counts in the queue for that instant; its start event, in the same instant, puts it on an agent.
  Closed forms (Erlang C, offered load a = 1.6, utilization rho = 0.8):
    probability a customer waits 0.7111      mean wait in the line Wq = 1.7778 minutes      mean time in the system W = 2.7778 minutes
    mean queue length Lq = lambda x Wq = 2.8444      mean busy agents = a = 1.6      mean number in the system L = 4.4444

  To run it:        grind with a count of 2000000 on samples/pro/process-flow-mmc.xml
  What to look for: the last row's column 5 is near 2.844 and column 6 near 1.6. The run starts with an empty desk, so a short run reads low. Consecutive
                    values are strongly correlated, so judge the average over several seeds, or over batches of rows, not over single rows.
  For waits per customer instead of events, set output="entities" on the ProcessFlow node and drop the Recur: one row per customer who has left, with the
  customer's wait, service and time in the system, where plain averages are right.
-->
<dataset columns="queueArea, busyArea, prevTime, prevQueue, prevBusy, meanQueue, meanBusy, time, customer, station, type, queue, busy, inSystem" seed="20261014">
  <root node="Averages"/>
  <nodes>
    <Exponential name="Gap" lambda="1.6"/>
    <Exponential name="Service" lambda="1.0"/>
    <ProcessFlow name="Desk" stations="Agent:2" entry="Agent">
      <input name="Gap" role="arrival"/>
      <input name="Service" role="service.Agent"/>
    </ProcessFlow>
    <Recur name="Averages" initial="0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0" appendInput="true"
           expression="y[0] = s[0] + s[3] * (x[0] - s[2]); y[1] = s[1] + s[4] * (x[0] - s[2]); y[2] = x[0]; y[3] = x[4]; y[4] = x[5]; y[5] = (s[0] + s[3] * (x[0] - s[2])) / x[0]; y[6] = (s[1] + s[4] * (x[0] - s[2])) / x[0]">
      <input name="Desk"/>
    </Recur>
  </nodes>
</dataset>
